    "growth_divergence_warning": "Equity 3yr CAGR 25.9% vs EPS 3yr 9.3% and revenue 3yr 14.1% -- divergence >30%; equity is not used as the growth anchor.",
    "notes": "Owner-FCF series (reported FCF - SBC - equity-award withholding taxes; interest income not stripped, company not materially net cash): FY2020 502.8, FY2022 736.4, FY2025 961.9. Owner-FCF 3yr CAGR 9.3% is 3.1pp below reported FCF 3yr CAGR 12.4%: SBC plus withholding grew from 382 (FY2022) to 625 (FY2025), so a rising share of reported growth is paid for in stock. FY2025 owner FCF is also depressed by the $140.6M export-control settlement. Revenue CAGRs include M&A (BETA CAE 2024, Hexagon D&E 2026)."
  },
  "scenario_narratives": {
    "base": "AI-driven custom-silicon demand (hyperscaler ASICs, chiplets/3D-IC) keeps Core EDA and IP growing low-teens near term on a record $8.2B backlog, Hexagon D&E is integrated into SD&A without further margin dilution, and the EDA oligopoly with Synopsys and Siemens holds pricing. Growth fades from 13% to 4% over the decade as the AI design cycle normalises; pre-SBC EBITDA margin recovers from the Hexagon dip to 47%; SBC plus withholding stays ~11-12% of revenue because talent cost is the business.",
    "bull": "The silicon-to-systems strategy works: BETA CAE plus Hexagon give Cadence a multiphysics/system-simulation franchise that competes head-on with Synopsys-Ansys, AI design tools (Cerebrus/Verisium, NVIDIA collaboration) are monetised as premium pricing rather than cannibalised, and China export licensing stays available under the trade truce. Mid-teens growth for five years, operating leverage takes pre-SBC EBITDA margin to 50%, and SBC intensity eases toward 10%.",
    "bear": "Two of the Analysis JSON's named risks bite together: China (~15-16% of revenue) is lost to renewed EDA export licensing and state-backed domestic EDA, while agentic AI design tools (the 'Kimi K3' scare) erode seat pricing and pull the semiconductor R&D cycle forward into a downturn after the AI capex peak. Growth falls to mid/high single digits, the Synopsys-Ansys combination presses SD&A pricing so Hexagon stays margin-dilutive (EBITDA drifts to 43%), and SBC stays ~12% as Cadence pays to retain engineers. Lower exit cap (15x) and 2.5% terminal growth."
  },
  "valuation_philosophy": {
    "summary": "House basis expenses SBC and the cash withheld on net-share settlement (~12% of revenue combined) and holds the diluted count flat; this is the dominant difference from street valuations of Cadence, which add SBC back. Flows are unlevered from a pre-SBC EBITDA build with negative working-capital capture, discounted at a 9% USD WACC over 10 years with a Gordon/cap terminal value.",
    "key_risks_to_valuation": [
      "Terminal value carries most of the value at 9% WACC and 3% growth; each 1pp of WACC moves value by roughly a fifth (see sensitivity).",
      "Serial M&A (BETA CAE, Hexagon $3.0B, Secure-IC, Arm Artisan IP) supports revenue growth but acquisition spend is not deducted from owner FCF -- if growth needs ~$0.5-1B/yr of deals, the model is generous.",
      "Cash tax rate is volatile (15%-37% of model EBIT across FY2023-2026) from Sec.174/OBBBA timing and Pillar 2; 26% is a through-cycle assumption.",
      "China export-control and DOJ-probation exposure (~15-16% of revenue).",
      "Agentic/general-purpose AI could compress EDA seat pricing over the terminal period.",
      "Combined SBC plus withholding (~12% of revenue) may not decline; treating it as non-cash roughly adds the street-basis premium shown in band_position."
    ]
  },
